{"name":"Silver Spoon","hex":"#d3d3d2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver spoon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Wealth passed down or inherited."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_spoon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver spoon","blocks":[{"text":"The English language expression silver spoon is synonymous with wealth, especially inherited wealth; someone born into a wealthy family is said to have \"been born with a silver spoon in their mouth\". As an adjective, \"silver spoon\" describes someone who has a prosperous background or is of a well-to-do family environment, often with the connotation that the person does not fully realize or appreciate the value of their advantage, its having been inherited rather than earned, hence the Australian term spooner for a young person so advantaged."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_spoon"},{"title":"Silver Spoon (manga)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Spoon  is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Hiromu Arakawa. It was serialized in Shogakukan's shōnen manga magazine Weekly Shōnen Sunday from April 2011 to November 2019. The story is set in the fictional Ooezo Agricultural High School in Hokkaido, and depicts the daily life of Yuugo Hachiken, a high school student from Sapporo who enrolled at the agricultural high school to escape the demands of his strict father. However, he soon learns that life on an agricultural school is not as easy as he initially believed. Unlike his new classmates, he has no intention of following an agricultural career after graduating, although he envies them for already having set goals for their lives and the pursuit of their dreams."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0d/Silver_Spoon_%28manga%29_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Spoon_(manga)"},{"title":"Spoon","blocks":[{"text":"A spoon is a utensil consisting of a shallow bowl, oval or round, at the end of a handle. A type of cutlery, especially as part of a place setting, it is used primarily for transferring food to the mouth (eating). Spoons are also used in food preparation to measure, mix, stir and toss ingredients and for serving food. Present day spoons are made from metal, wood, porcelain or plastic. There are many different types of spoons made from different materials by different cultures for different purposes and food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Soup_Spoon.jpg/330px-Soup_Spoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spoon"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg/500px-Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg/1280px-Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg/500px-Folding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Folding silver spoon, 18th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFolding_spoon_MET_17701.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/09/Silver_Spoon_characters.png/330px-Silver_Spoon_characters.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/09/Silver_Spoon_characters.png/330px-Silver_Spoon_characters.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The main characters of the Silver Spoon series. From left to right: Tokiwa, Inada, Yoshino, Hachiken, Mikage, Nishikawa, Komaba, Aikawa, and Beppu.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_Spoon_characters.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/History_spoon.jpg/500px-History_spoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/History_spoon.jpg/500px-History_spoon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sasanian spoon and fork (4th century)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistory_spoon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Catalogue_of_the_silver_plate_%28Greek%2C_Etruscan_and_Roman%29_in_the_British_Museum_%281921%29_%2814764117764%29.jpg/500px-Catalogue_of_the_silver_plate_%28Greek%2C_Etruscan_and_Roman%29_in_the_British_Museum_%281921%29_%2814764117764%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Catalogue_of_the_silver_plate_%28Greek%2C_Etruscan_and_Roman%29_in_the_British_Museum_%281921%29_%2814764117764%29.jpg/500px-Catalogue_of_the_silver_plate_%28Greek%2C_Etruscan_and_Roman%29_in_the_British_Museum_%281921%29_%2814764117764%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi